WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.164
Module three:

2
00:00:01.164 --> 00:00:02.543
centralized exchanges.

3
00:00:02.543 --> 00:00:07.429
How the order book works, what fees really cost, what futures and funding are,

4
00:00:07.429 --> 00:00:11.438
and why leverage liquidates you even when the forecast is right.

5
00:00:12.438 --> 00:00:14.627
Nobody sets the price on an exchange.

6
00:00:14.627 --> 00:00:15.988
There is an order book:

7
00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:18.414
a list of those who want to buy and sell.

8
00:00:18.414 --> 00:00:21.076
Sellers are at the top, buyers at the bottom,

9
00:00:21.076 --> 00:00:24.034
and the gap between the best prices is the spread.

10
00:00:24.034 --> 00:00:26.460
A limit order sits in the book and waits.

11
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.134
A market order takes the best orders one after another until it fills its size.

12
00:00:32.134 --> 00:00:35.109
In the lesson you can get your hands on the order book:

13
00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:38.300
send a market order and see at what average price it fills.

14
00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:42.519
The bigger the order compared with the depth of the book, the worse the price.

15
00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:43.438
That is slippage.

16
00:00:44.438 --> 00:00:47.740
Whoever's order waited in the book is the maker.

17
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.455
Whoever took it is the taker, and the taker pays more.

18
00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:55.719
On spot the base fee is about one tenth of a percent per side,

19
00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.402
and a “buy — sell” round is two tenths.

20
00:00:58.402 --> 00:00:59.365
It seems tiny.

21
00:00:59.365 --> 00:01:05.006
But 500 rounds turn 100 dollars into about 37, without a single forecasting error.

22
00:01:06.006 --> 00:01:09.677
Hence a rule that honest testing confirms again and again:

23
00:01:09.677 --> 00:01:11.576
the more often an idea trades,

24
00:01:11.576 --> 00:01:14.994
the bigger the edge it needs just to pay for the fees.

25
00:01:14.994 --> 00:01:19.867
An idea that buys and sells with market orders every day loses about half its

26
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:21.893
capital in a year on fees alone.

27
00:01:21.893 --> 00:01:25.374
That is why the program deducts costs from every trade.

28
00:01:26.374 --> 00:01:28.410
On spot you buy the coin itself.

29
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.700
A future is a contract on its price:

30
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.009
there is no coin, only settlement of the difference.

31
00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:38.590
A dated future has a date by which the gap to the coin price disappears.

32
00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:42.598
A perpetual has no date, and funding ties it to the coin price.

33
00:01:43.598 --> 00:01:46.895
Funding is a regular payment between longs and shorts.

34
00:01:46.895 --> 00:01:49.460
When the crowd wants to buy with leverage,

35
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.024
the rate is positive and longs pay shorts.

36
00:01:52.024 --> 00:01:56.298
One hundredth of a percent per eight hours is about 11 percent a year,

37
00:01:56.298 --> 00:01:59.534
and in hot periods the rate can be many times higher.

38
00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:05.459
Leverage of 10 lets you open a 1,000-dollar position with 100 of collateral.

39
00:02:05.459 --> 00:02:09.217
A one percent price move is ten percent of the collateral.

40
00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:12.198
When the loss has almost eaten the collateral,

41
00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.790
the exchange closes the position itself.

42
00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.864
The main trap is the price path:

43
00:02:16.864 --> 00:02:20.687
after a 12 percent fall the position is already liquidated,

44
00:02:20.687 --> 00:02:23.214
and a 20 percent rise will not help it.

45
00:02:24.214 --> 00:02:29.168
With isolated margin, liquidation takes only the collateral of that position.

46
00:02:29.168 --> 00:02:32.449
With cross margin, the whole account is collateral:

47
00:02:32.449 --> 00:02:37.274
liquidation is pushed further away, but a bad position can take everything.

48
00:02:37.274 --> 00:02:42.743
And liquidation is calculated on the mark price, an average across several exchanges,

49
00:02:42.743 --> 00:02:47.182
so that one random spike does not close everyone's positions at once.

50
00:02:48.182 --> 00:02:49.185
The main points.

51
00:02:49.185 --> 00:02:50.878
The price is an order book:

52
00:02:50.878 --> 00:02:53.637
a market order pays the spread and slippage.

53
00:02:53.637 --> 00:02:55.581
The fee for a round seems tiny,

54
00:02:55.581 --> 00:03:00.346
but with frequent trading it eats capital faster than any forecasting error.

55
00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:04.923
And leverage of 10 liquidates on a move of about ten percent against you,

56
00:03:04.923 --> 00:03:07.118
even if the price later comes back.
